course: dictionary
lesson: 1561
title: Chaos in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Chaos is a noun meaning complete confusion and disorder, where nothing is organised. Think of the morning rush when shoes, bags and toast all go missing at once. «The bathroom queue at 7:30 is chaos.» Here chaos means a scene where everyone is pushing and nothing runs smoothly.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The bathroom queue at 7:30 is confusion and disorder» still makes sense, so you have chosen well. Context tells you what the word means in each sentence.
explain: Chaos also has two special meanings in other subjects. In science, tiny changes at the start can lead to huge, hard-to-predict differences later, as in weather. In old myths, Chaos was the formless emptiness before the world was made. The same word can mean different things, so look at the subject around it.
fact: Chaos is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some chaos, not a chaos.
fact: «Chaos» means complete confusion and disorder, where nothing is organised.
fact: In science, chaos means tiny changes at the start can lead to huge, hard-to-predict differences later, which is why weather forecasts get less certain the further ahead they look.
fact: In old Greek myths, Chaos came first, before the gods, as a formless emptiness.
fact: «SNOW CHAOS!» in a headline means snow has thrown travel and everyday life into disorder.
exercise: «The bathroom queue at 7:30 is chaos.» What does chaos mean here? | A bathroom with a new tap | A type of cold weather | *Complete confusion and disorder | A long, calm line of people
exercise: In science, chaos means tiny changes at the start lead to huge, hard-to-predict differences later. Which example fits best? | A bell rings at the same time each morning | A book has one hundred pages | *Weather forecasts get less certain the further ahead they look | A plant grows one centimetre every day
exercise: Find and copy the one word from «In Harrogate, Yorkshire, Pip's three cousins stay for a week of summer: water fights, bunk beds, chips every night, absolute chaos.» that means complete disorder. | *chaos | cousins | chips | week
test: In old Greek stories, what was Chaos? | A kind of bread | *The formless emptiness before the world was made | A small village in Yorkshire | A fierce god who ruled the sea
test: Wren's friend says that people without writing had no laws, just chaos. What does this suggest? | Life with many laws and rules | Life near the sea | *Life without rules or order | Life with lots of writing
exam: Swap chaos for a word with the same meaning in «The morning rush is chaos.» | Sunshine | *Disorder | Silence | Calm
